# Core engineering

## Change discipline

- Understand outcome, module, and existing conventions before editing.
- Smallest coherent change that meets acceptance criteria.
- Do not invent APIs, entities, events, config keys, or rules without marking assumptions.
- Search for existing patterns before new abstractions.
- When unverified, say exactly what remains unverified.

## No hardcoding

- No magic URLs, connection strings, keys, queue/exchange names, model IDs, timeouts, roles, or env hosts in domain/application code.
- Use typed Options, named policies, value objects, SharedKernel primitives.
- Secrets never in source, messages, prompts, traces, or logs.

## Clean code

- Domain vocabulary; one level of abstraction per method; early returns.
- Comments explain *why*; no drive-by refactors.
- Immutable command/result records; explicit results for expected business failures.

## Pragmatic SOLID

Apply when it removes real coupling or enables a real substitution. Do not manufacture interfaces/layers “for SOLID”.

## Defaults

- UTC inside; locale only at UI edge.
- Timeout every external call; retry only transient + idempotent.
- Tests at lowest useful level for new behavior.
- GoF only with named variation (see ADR-0012).
